[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:44] Take a minute to lay out your full structure first. Is that framework MECE — what's missing?

[candidate · 3:14] Fair push. Let me tighten it.

Top level: profit = revenue − cost. That's MECE by definition. The gap is I jumped straight to aggregate; I should separate per-store economics from store count, because 200 locations growing could mask per-store decline.

So: revenue = number of stores × transactions per store × average ticket. Ticket itself = price × items per basket, and I'd want mix inside that — drinks versus food, in-store versus delivery apps, since delivery carries commission.

Costs I'd split fixed versus variable. Variable: COGS — beans, dairy, cups — plus hourly labor and any delivery commission. Fixed: rent, salaried management, corporate SG&A, marketing, D&A on buildouts.

What I left out entirely: external factors. Commodity prices on coffee and dairy, wage legislation, competitive pricing pressure. Those sit outside the client's control but drive the cost lines.

Also non-operating: interest expense if they levered up to fund expansion, and tax rate — net margin includes both.

Can I get the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue, both years?
