[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 6:23] You've assumed prices didn't move to get there. Here's the menu price change: Menu prices have not changed in two years.

[candidate · 7:53] Good, that confirms it. So the root cause is pass-through failure, not an operational cost problem. Costs inflated roughly 40% on the input side and the menu hasn't moved once.

And note what that does to the revenue growth: 15% growth with flat prices means it's all volume or new stores, so they've been selling more units at a worse margin on each one. Growth was actually making it worse.

Let me size the fix. To get COGS back from 58 to 42 points, I need revenue per unit up enough that the same cost dollars are a smaller share. Roughly, if costs per unit are fixed, price needs to rise by 58/42 — about 1.38, so call it a 38% price increase to fully restore. That's a lot.

But I don't need full restoration. To get margin from 6% to, say, 15%, I need about 9 points — call it a 10% price rise, since each 1% of price drops roughly 1 point of COGS share. Do we have any elasticity data, or what competitors have done on price?
