[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 7:54] Walk me through that last step — where does one percent of price giving roughly one point of COGS share come from?

[candidate · 9:24] Let me actually check that, because I think I hand-waved it.

If I hold cost dollars fixed at 58 and raise price 10%, revenue goes to 110. COGS share is 58/110, about 53%. So that's a 5 point drop in COGS share, not 10. My one-for-one rule was wrong.

But the margin math still works, because labor and overhead shrink as a share too. Total costs are 94 today. Raise price 10%: revenue 110, costs still 94, profit 16 on 110 — call it 14.5%. So a 10% price rise does get me from 6% to roughly 14–15%. I got the right answer through the wrong reasoning.

Cleaner rule: every 1% of price adds a bit under 1 point of margin at these cost levels, because nearly all costs are fixed in dollar terms against it. That's assuming zero volume loss, which is the thing I'd want elasticity for.
