[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:27] Understood. On elasticity — I don't have that breakdown for you. So beyond price, what else could the client do to reverse this? Give me a structured set of options.

[candidate · 10:57] No elasticity, so I'll assume specialty coffee is moderately inelastic given competitors face the same commodity inflation — I'd flag that as the key thing to test.

Three buckets beyond straight price.

One, revenue per ticket without a headline price rise. Upsell and mix shift toward higher-margin items — food attach, larger sizes, loyalty bundles. Less visible to customers than a menu-board increase.

Two, input cost. Hedge or lock forward contracts on beans and dairy, since roughly 40% inflation was the whole problem. Renegotiate supplier terms at 200-store scale. Reduce waste and tighten portioning — that's the piece I can't size without data on waste rates.

Three, the overhead and growth question. Overhead's only up 2 points, so it's not the story, but 15% revenue growth on flat prices means they added volume or stores. If new stores are dilutive, slowing expansion protects margin.

Ranking: price is the lever that actually matches the size of the gap. The rest are supporting.
