[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:58] Which of those would you sequence first, and why?

[candidate · 12:28] Price first, but phased rather than one jump.

Reason is size. The gap is 18 points of margin and about 16 of it is COGS inflation. Hedging protects against the next 40%, it doesn't recover the last two years. Waste and mix might get a point or two. Only price is the same order of magnitude as the problem.

I'd sequence it as: raise prices roughly 10% over two or three increments across a few quarters — call it 3 to 4% a step — and test in a subset of stores first, maybe 20 of the 200, to read actual volume response since we don't have elasticity. That gets to roughly 14–15% margin if volume holds.

In parallel, immediately: lock forward contracts on beans and dairy. That's low regret and stops the bleeding from getting worse while the price test runs.

Third, waste and upsell — genuinely useful but slower and smaller, so it runs behind.

Slowing store expansion I'd hold until we know whether new stores are actually dilutive.
