[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:30] The CEO steps into the room and can give you two minutes. What's your recommendation?

[candidate · 14:01] Brew & Bean should raise menu prices by roughly 10%, phased over two to three quarters. That's the recommendation.

Here's why. Margin fell 18 points, from 24 to 6. Sixteen of those 18 points — call it 90% — come from COGS, which went from 42% of revenue to 58%. Labor is flat. Overhead moved 2 points. Input costs inflated about 40% across beans, dairy, and packaging, and menu prices haven't moved once in two years. So this isn't an operations problem. You've absorbed the entire commodity shock.

Worse, the 15% revenue growth came from volume, not price, so every incremental cup sold at these costs has been diluting margin further.

On the math: 10% price, costs held flat in dollars, gets margin to roughly 14–15%.

Risks. I don't have elasticity, so test in 20 stores before a national rollout. And competitors face the same inflation, so watch whether they've already moved.

Immediately and in parallel: lock forward contracts on beans and dairy so this can't repeat.

Next steps — price test, competitor price benchmark, and a read on whether new stores are margin-dilutive.
